From: xnulinux
Subject: [PATCH] linux64: up to 32 CPUs

The virtual board's kernel was built for 8 CPUs (MAX_CPUS, the SM8550's and SM8350's eight cores), and xnu.ko gave
XNU up to 16. A host asked for 12 ended the boot: the device tree's topology stopped at 8, XnuCPU registered the
ninth CPU, and ml_processor_register read CpuDataEntries[8] (cpu_datap) before its own range check: a data abort at
address 0 ("XNU took a fault it cannot handle", pc in _ml_processor_register).

MAX_CPUS is now 32, and LINUX64_MAX_CPUS (linux64_abi.h) names the same limit for the host: xnu.ko gives XNU no more,
and a static assertion here keeps the two together. ml_processor_register checks the CPU's number before it reads the
entry, so a CPU beyond the topology is refused (KERN_FAILURE, which XnuCPU logs) instead of faulting.
---
--- a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/board_config.h
+++ b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/board_config.h
@@ -308,7 +308,12 @@
 #include <pexpert/arm64/BCM2837.h>
 
 #define MAX_L2_CLINE                   6
-#define MAX_CPUS                       8 /* SM8550 (sheng) and SM8350 (zeta) have eight cores */
+/*
+ * LINUX64 (xnulinux): as many as the hosted system can be given (LINUX64_MAX_CPUS in linux64_abi.h, which
+ * machine_routines.c checks against this). With 8, the SM8550's and SM8350's eight cores, a host that gave XNU more
+ * ended the boot: XnuCPU registered the ninth, and ml_processor_register read past CpuDataEntries.
+ */
+#define MAX_CPUS                       32
 /*
  * Two clusters: the device tree's cpus carry cluster-type 'E' or 'P' from the host's cpu capacities (make-droidvm-adt.py; all
  * 'P' when the host gives every vCPU the same). The AMP scheduler (sched_amp, not Edge: Edge places threads by CLPC's thread group
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
@@ -1527,6 +1527,8 @@
 }
 
 static_assert(MAX_CPUS <= 256, "MAX_CPUS must fit in _COMM_PAGE_CPU_TO_CLUSTER; Increase table size if needed");
+/* LINUX64 (xnulinux): the host gives XNU up to LINUX64_MAX_CPUS CPUs (xnu.ko), and the per-CPU tables must hold them */
+static_assert(MAX_CPUS >= LINUX64_MAX_CPUS, "MAX_CPUS (board_config.h) is below LINUX64_MAX_CPUS (linux64_abi.h)");
 
 void
 ml_map_cpus_to_clusters(uint8_t *table)
@@ -1690,6 +1692,14 @@
     processor_t *processor_out, ipi_handler_t *ipi_handler_out,
     perfmon_interrupt_handler_func *pmi_handler_out)
 {
+	/*
+	 * LINUX64 (xnulinux): a CPU the topology does not have is refused before its entry is read. The check below came
+	 * after cpu_datap(), which reads CpuDataEntries[log_id]: a device tree with more CPUs than MAX_CPUS (a host
+	 * giving XNU more than it was built for) took a data abort here instead of KERN_FAILURE.
+	 */
+	if (in_processor_info->log_id > (uint32_t)ml_get_max_cpu_number()) {
+		return KERN_FAILURE;
+	}
 	cpu_data_t *this_cpu_datap = cpu_datap(in_processor_info->log_id);
 	assert3u(this_cpu_datap->cpu_number, ==, in_processor_info->log_id); /* from ml_bootstrap_processors() */
 
